WEBVTT

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:30.750
Thank you.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:39.180
and beef that's the stuff that makes soldiers besides it gives a man a figure have you seen

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:45.580
today's time sir hello will you the news from paris yes over 50 people guillotined in one day

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.500
damnable useless cruelty oh well what can you expect of a lot of foreigners with no sporting

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.560
things again if it wasn't for our fox hunting and our pheasant shooting i dare say we should

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.820
be cruel too let's hope the scarlet pimple will save some of the rest do you know who he is

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.880
But I confess I feel a little prouder.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:04.890
And I remember that he is an Englishman.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.850
Well, whoever he is, he'll have his work cut out in Paris.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:09.860
Will you read this, sir?

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:54.550
the same drums roll the knife falls and another else secret thoughts can't believe it's here

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.710
and so clumsy that great ugly machine

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:06.160
All they need is a sharp razor, a quick wrist and flick.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.710
Yes, steady. Oh, you're all right, Father.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.220
I wish they'd give me a few aristocrats to shave.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:13.260
Asia.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.420
Then we should see some sport.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.200
You would.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.570
You see me dealing with the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:19.070
The what?

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:20.430
The Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:21.760
Haven't you heard?

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.370
There's a band of Englishmen helping these cursed aristos out of the country.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.900
The chief was the only man who ever cheated Madame Guilty.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.320
He's known by the title of the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:35.940
Who is he?

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.860
Ah, that's the mystery.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:19.310
Got ya?

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:20.090
Yes.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.940
And you thought you could fool me with a disguise like that.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:37.960
Well, I'll keep this wig of yours,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.360
because after tomorrow, you won't have a head to put it on.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.500
I shall be delighted to visit Madame Guillotine

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.040
and make the acquaintance of the only decent person left in France.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.180
Captain, I say death to the Republic, and long live the King!

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.390
That makes the third aristocrat I've caught this week.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.280
I can smell them through any disguise.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:51.380
for the game of chess it enables us to forget the more disagreeable realities of life i'm not so

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:57.000
sure it is a good thing we've been too detached from reality all our lives that's what caused

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:57.540
the revolution.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:58.600
Possibly.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:08:59.540
Undoubtedly.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.240
If we'd only had eyes to see our own follies, we shouldn't be here now,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.420
waiting to be shaved by the national razor.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.120
Which is the Count de Tournay?

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.120
I'm the Count de Tournay's daughter.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:14.430
My father's over there playing the chair.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:16.260
This is my mother.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:29.530
Count de Tournay, passage I have marked.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:31.430
Your spirits will be uplifted.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.190
We're not afraid to die, father.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:35.840
Even so, it is sometimes better to live.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.930
...64. Robespierre gives Madame Guillotine extra fodder today.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:29.150
The former Duke de Tours, the former Abbé de Saint-Preux, the former Count and Countess de Lanay, the former Madame de Nijon, the former Count de Tarré, the former Marquess de Jouvence and Family, the former Marquess de Riboupierre, the two Countesses Erie, the former Mademoiselle de Bonnard.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.030
the two former duchesses, Passé and Bonnevaux,

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.500
the former Marquis de Marais,

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.670
the former Count de Tournai and family,

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.490
the former Countess de Cherreville,

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:47.110
the former Madame de Picard,

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.740
the former Count d'Arblay.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:12.150
Are you an attorney?

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:12.970
I am.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:14.500
You're remanded.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.940
Citizen Robespierre wants to see you.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:17.600
My God!

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.400
Unless you get on!

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:19.680
Take him inside!

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:20.480
Go on!

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:21.320
Go on!

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:01.850
He's never failed to keep his penis.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.140
Too late.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:21.970
King of France!

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:27.370
King of France!

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:24.290
Look at it, 20 locks of hair in it.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:28.070
There's the Duke of Marie.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:29.960
There's his handsome...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.170
Weiss, the duchess. And here's the hair of our lover, a handsome man. Where's your passport,

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:45.630
hag? He takes me for an aristocrat. You're right, captain. Can't be too careful, can you? No,

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:54.310
you can't be too... Here's my passport. This says citizeness, Renault, and grandson.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:59.900
Where's the grandson? He's in the cart, poor darling. What's the matter?

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:09.920
I don't think I shall get him alive as far as Charles Beauvoir.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:13.330
Give me a light from your part when it happens.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.670
Keep away from me, will you, plague? Get out of here, quick!

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.590
And when you come back to Paris, try another gate,

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.110
for you won't pass through this one again.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:23.340
You're right, Captain. You're right. But I'll be back, somehow.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:53.900
An old hag of a woman will pass this way in a cart. You're to stop her and arrest her.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:55.860
Did you say an old hag?

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:57.320
You heard me.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.790
But she passed through here sometimes.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:05.320
Oh, miserable fool! You blundering idiot! Did you search the cart?

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:11.180
No, no, I... Her grandson was inside with the plague. Would you expect me to expose...

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:14.540
You were expected to use your brains! Now I see you haven't any.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.680
That sick grandson was rarely three aristocrats snatched from the guillotine.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.760
Ten thousand hell. And the old hag, who was she?

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:27.980
The old hag, my clever captain. Was that cursed Englishman the scarlet pimper now?

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:29.960
Forward, we'll catch...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.480
This time

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:44.370
It seems like a troop of horsemen.

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:49.070
We are being pursued.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:22.640
friends these soldiers my friends mademoiselle and yours are you the scarlet pimperdale i'm only a

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:27.790
humble instrument in the hands of our great leader how many are you 20 madame one to command 19 to

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:29.970
obey shall we see your leader i'm sorry i must ask you

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.030
Where was the tourney?

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:05.990
Taken back into prison.

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:06.510
Why?

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:07.290
We don't know.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:08.530
Then we have to come back and fetch him.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:10.080
How are things at Calais?

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:11.680
Lawson's there, and Ashurst.

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:12.760
He hadn't arrived when he left.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:13.320
Oh, hadn't he?

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.060
His time sense is defective, that's twice.

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:16.180
They may have arrested him.

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:17.980
They're very careful at the channel ports nowadays.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:19.900
My orders to you are that you're not to be arrested.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.600
And I expect my orders to be obeyed.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:22.280
Yes, Percy.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:24.850
You, Wilmot, and Grant go back to Paris.

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:25.990
My brother-in-law will follow you there.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:28.920
All three of you get your next instructions from Quentin.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:29.790
Well...

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.660
Goodbye, and remember, don't get caught.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:33.500
Right, right.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:34.920
Good luck. Bye.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:35.870
Go!

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:03.270
Pierre wants to see him because the ambassador of the Republic has come over from London.

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:04.190
Chauvelin?

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:05.090
Why?

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:06.830
All in your honour, Percy.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.760
Your work is becoming even more dangerous than it was.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.240
Our work, Armand.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.330
You go back to Paris at once.

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:16.770
Find out everything you can about Chauvelin.

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:18.550
If he goes to London, you go too.

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:23.540
Our most dangerous enemy is Citizen Chauvelin.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:47.250
My dear Chauvelin, I'm very sorry that I had to ask you to undertake the journey from London,

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.980
but this matter of the Scarlet Pimpernel has become a national menace.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:57.000
How can you expect me to detect him in a country that hates us like the devil

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.580
and puts every obstacle in my way?

00:19:58.700 --> 00:19:59.960
We expect you to overcome...

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:05.600
Like our generals in the field, they know that to lose a battle means the guillotine.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:07.550
Have you any plans?

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:10.380
Naturally.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:15.720
We may assume that the Scarlet Pimpernin of this gang speak perfect French.

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:21.140
They belong, therefore, to a class that has been brought up by French nurses and French tutors.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:22.360
So far, not bad.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.860
I have a list of all the likely names.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.000
I have them watched day and night.

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:29.060
Sooner or later, I draw the net.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:29.840
You think?

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:31.090
De Tournay might be useful?

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:32.270
Possible.

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:37.990
De Tournay.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:45.700
Former Count de Tournay.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.700
Former ambassador of the former King of France in London.

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:50.390
Citizen Chauvelin.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.500
Ambassador of the French Republic in London.

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:54.770
De Tournay, I want to make you an offer.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:57.100
We offer you your life.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:20:59.600
All we ask in return.

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:02.630
is that you merely get in touch with your many English friends

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:06.770
and soon you will be able to tell us who is the man known as the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:08.320
You want me to be a spy?

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.740
You've been too long in politics not to be a realist.

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:15.120
A little piece of information, we give you your life.

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:18.940
God gave me my life and he will take it away as soon as it pleases him.

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:24.080
But now my family has gone to the guillotine, my one wishes to follow them.

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:29.840
Alter your outlook to hear that your family is alive.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:31.350
Don't trifle with me.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:34.400
I send them to the guillotine for the future happiness of the human race.

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.120
But I do not allow torture.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.880
Where are they? I saw them being dragged towards the tumble.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.020
On their way to the guillotine, the band of the scarlet pimpernel snatched them away.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.110
Where are they? Probably in England by this time.

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:59.970
His Majesty King George III. God bless him for his...

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:07.770
To King Louis of France. May God protect him.

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:13.330
And to your husband, madam.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:18.200
I hope the Scarlet Pimphenel, a new gentleman, will rescue him, as you did us.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:22.870
Scarlet Pimphenel. What a joel name. What does it mean?

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.070
It is a humble wayside flower, mademoiselle,

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:28.710
and it hides the identity of the best and bravest man in all the world.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:29.980
Do you know, sir, I...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.560
and who my best friend is in London, Marguerite St. Juste.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:34.220
Suzanne, how awful am I to tell you

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:36.460
I will not have that woman's name mentioned in my presence.

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:38.780
But, Mother, you can't believe that dreadful story.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:39.740
What story?

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:40.820
It's the truth.

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:43.840
Marguerite St. Juste denounced the Marquis de Saint-Cyr

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:45.180
and his family to the tribunal.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:46.900
They all went to the guillotine.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:48.960
Marguerite St. Juste?

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:51.610
Yes. She married an Englishman.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:52.880
Do you know her?

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:54.120
Know her?

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:56.720
Everybody in London knows Lady Blakeney.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:22.170
something in your mouth? Not now, Mr. Romney. I can't do justice to that lovely face when it's

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:29.870
all bulged out with bonbons. You may talk if you like, but no bonbons. And no gestures, please. All right.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:37.440
I'll tell you what it is about the Pimpernel that tantalises me so.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:41.020
This is insufferable modesty. There's no excuse for it.

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:44.460
It's maddening to think that somewhere there's a man as marvellous as that.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.090
And we never see him. He's of no use to us, whatever.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:50.030
Lady Blakeney, I said no gestures, please.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:55.080
You must remember that I'm a finchwoman and when I talk I must use these as well as this.

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:06.060
You'd have two and a family arrived in Dover today.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:07.340
All except the father.

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:08.920
We'll have to go back for him.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:11.680
It's going to be devilish awkward after what's happened.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:14.120
Well, I'll go, and so will Hastings, if you'll let us.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:15.640
We'll all go, if it comes to that.

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:17.590
But even so, we're only 20.

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:20.030
Yesterday, 30 people went to the guillotine.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.090
Are you trying to suggest there aren't enough of us?

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:25.010
Percy, if there isn't a decent Englishman wouldn't be proud to join us.

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:27.640
We could be 500, 1,000 within a week.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:29.970
And within another week, every one of us would be...

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.460
a marked man. We shouldn't be able to rescue a single soul.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:37.620
Mere force is useless against people who are neither cowards nor fools.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:41.190
We must match courage with courage and cunning with still greater

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:44.680
cunning if we're to do anything at all. We're all willing to give our lives

00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:48.000
but we must do more than that. We must mask our identities.

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:53.650
Suffer the humiliation of being taken for fox, fools, nitwits, cowards.

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:57.260
So many people die every day that could be saved.

00:24:57.850 --> 00:24:59.980
Do you think I don't feel that? Do you think...

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.930
A guy like sitting there in the shadow of the knife, while one head falls after another.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:07.870
People I know and love, innocent people, kindly people,

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.610
herded like sheep, butchered like cattle,

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:16.180
by men who make high-sounding principles an excuse for the most bestial cruelty,

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.360
robes Pierre's liberty, his equality.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:27.850
Sink me if it isn't Colonel Remsbottom.

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.350
Well, who, Ramsbottom?

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:34.370
Winterbottom, sir!

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:39.710
Ramsbottom, what a ridiculous mistake!

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:44.590
Allow me to explain, Colonel.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:46.810
Here's that ass, Blakely.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:59.970
Oh, God's blood, this club.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.400
No one's cheering up. Sink me, the place is a mausoleum.

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.350
Are you being offensive, sir? Who, sir? Me, sir? No, sir.

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.880
Stap me, I'm bubbling over with good humor this morning.

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:13.260
Would you believe me? I've just written a masterpiece.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.180
Who, sir? You, sir? Me, sir. No, sir!

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.870
Yes, sir. All about this mysterious Pimpernel fellow.

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:21.600
Oh, it came to me, heaven only knows, because it was the busiest moment of the day.

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:22.980
Damn me, I was tying my cravat.

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:29.720
Oh, well, here it is. The Scarlet Pimpernel by Sir Percival Blakeney Baronet.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.930
Well, that's the title. They seek him here.

00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:36.630
They seek him there. Those Frenchies seek him everywhere.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.390
Is he in heaven? Is he in hell?

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.300
That damned, elusive Pimpernel.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:45.540
What? Not bad.

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.930
Not bad. It's damn good. I'll write it out.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.330
Sink me, then. You can all learn it.

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:53.050
Yes. Well, good day, gentlemen.

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:55.200
And goodbye, Higgybottom.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:56.800
Winterbottom, curse you!

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:58.980
I beg your pardon. Didn't I say Winterbottom?

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:09.070
jackin' apes like these fashionable puppies insufferable what that young man needs is a

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:18.380
year or two's hard campaigning facing powder shot undergoing privation i can't imagine blakeney

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:28.140
running into much danger not he he doesn't belong to our vintage it's a matter of infinitely small

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:29.880
importance to me whose fault it is

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:30.940
Sorry, sir.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.000
The man who could put starch in my jabot

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:36.280
is equally capable of putting poison in my coffee.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:42.360
There, you see? I'm a laughingstock, and rightly so.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:46.060
Look at the cursed thing, sticking out like a barber's pole.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.240
Sink me, the thing's a disaster.

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.410
Go away, Brinker, you dreadful fellow.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:57.740
Ladies, your servant, Marguerite, your slave.

00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:06.260
Ah, Runley. How are you? And how is the portrait progressing?

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:19.100
The eyes are wrong, aren't they? And is the nose all right?

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:27.230
Well, I rather think you've missed the mouth altogether.

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:29.980
Yes, you...

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:33.330
Otherwise, it's the image of her.

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:36.940
I think I should be enough for the day.

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:40.270
Go on, Mr. Romney. You're tired and so am I.

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:42.350
Tired? Me too.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:46.890
You know, dear lady, I've just been to Bath to be cured of the fatigue.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:54.900
And now I'm so fatigued by the cure that I really think I should have to go back to Bath again to be cured of the fatigue.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:01.320
Bye.

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:03.040
Bye.

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:03.360
Bye.

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:20.680
Bye.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:21.120
Bye.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:54.580
Whatever. There's something lacking.

00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:55.960
What?

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.750
I don't know. There's a look.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:03.680
Look in the eyes. He's lost it.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:07.800
Perhaps I've lost it.

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:13.290
If it was happiness.

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:16.130
Good gad, my dear.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:18.380
How you could be unhappy, I can't imagine.

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:20.160
Can't you?

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:23.440
Percy, can't you?

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:25.090
Oh, cursed if I can.

00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:28.400
Why, you're the most courted woman in town.

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:29.200
By whom?

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:29.980
By...

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:31.310
Except you.

00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:33.730
Dear me, my dear, I'm your husband.

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:38.180
Months after we were married, we were still happy.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:43.770
And then came this estrangement, which heaven knows is none of my making.

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:45.860
Can you honestly say that?

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:48.050
Can you honestly deny that you've changed?

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:51.220
Changed?

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:52.780
I scarcely know you.

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:55.520
You're never with me now.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.100
You're always away on some pretext or other.

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:03.560
I'm always alone.

00:31:08.930 --> 00:31:11.230
Then I've mighty good news for you, my dear.

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:13.720
Your brother's arriving from Paris.

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:14.810
Armand?

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:15.330
Yes.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:16.340
When?

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:17.570
At any moment.

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:20.860
And then you won't be alone, will you?

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:26.050
When am I going to see you again?

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:27.430
Whenever you wish to.

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:29.340
Well then, every day.

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:06.630
Where's the chief?

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:11.210
He went straight up to town yesterday as soon as he arrived here.

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:12.150
Any orders for us?

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:12.530
Yes.

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:15.550
We're to wait here till tonight for a message from Armand St. Juste.

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:15.950
Right.

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:28.790
All of them?

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:29.700
Yes.

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:37.330
sailor we have a letter to surrender folks did you get it it wasn't easy but we had six men there

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:56.080
where are they now gagged and bound on the ship in the letter release them tomorrow watch every movement

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:13.030
Mayer, go over to France at once.

00:33:13.130 --> 00:33:14.610
The moment Sanchez returns, arrest him.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:22.240
Agreed, Sanchez.

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:25.800
Famous actress.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:29.850
She will give a great performance by...

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.720
for her brother's sake.

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:37.810
Percy, I don't want Armand to go back to France.

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:40.010
If you help me, we can keep him in England.

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:43.770
He'll settle down here, marry a nice girl,

00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:45.470
and have lovely children.

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:48.170
What a dreadful prospect.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:51.840
What has poor Armand done to be sentenced to matrimony?

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:54.640
You should know better, my dear.

00:34:10.750 --> 00:34:11.870
You're unhappy. Why?

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:19.560
Armand, I have the satisfaction of knowing that the biggest fool in England has the most complete contempt for his wife.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:21.480
Percy? How did it happen?

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:29.230
I don't know. I only know that when I married him, he was a man.

00:34:30.250 --> 00:34:34.000
He was my lover. I was glad to give up everything for him.

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:42.980
And now? You mustn't leave me, Armand. I need you. I'm all alone.

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:47.210
I can't stay. You're going back to France? But it's not safe.

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:50.460
For me? I'm no aristocrat. For anyone, under the terror.

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:52.800
I have to look after the Blakeney estate in France.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:55.450
Oh, Percy's so rich. What does it mean to him?

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:58.820
He's not even interested in his English estates.

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:03.280
He wouldn't go over to France for all the treasures in the world.

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:05.730
Why should you risk your life for his money?

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:09.500
Oh, I don't risk my life. Don't worry, darling.

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:27.260
May I ask you something? What's happened to you and Marguerite?

00:35:31.250 --> 00:35:34.140
Why? You loved her once.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:39.100
I know. Do you remember the first family to go to the guillotine?

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:44.090
The Saint-Cyrs. They told me it was Marguerite who denounced them.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:48.290
It was a lie. I wish I could believe that.

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:50.680
Did you ask her? I did.

00:35:52.070 --> 00:35:55.350
She flashed back a yes as sharp as the knife of the guillotine.

00:35:58.740 --> 00:35:59.700
Now watch that execution.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:07.770
The Marquis, his wife, his son, and it was my wife.

00:36:09.220 --> 00:36:09.780
Put them there.

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:14.620
So that's why you ceased to love her.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:16.880
What a tragedy.

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:21.660
Ceased, I shall love her till I die.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:23.920
That's the tragedy.

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:29.820
To make up for what she did to them.

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:33.830
...that you risk your own life, week after week, to rescue the others.

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:39.620
Don't be so heroic, Almond. I got a smack in the eye, and I took refuge in sport.

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:44.950
And what a sport. I can't. I think I shall give up fox hunting this winter.

00:36:55.750 --> 00:36:59.970
What do you think of it? Monstrous find, sir. Vastly becoming...

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:03.200
Now, there's a coat that you can wear with comfort, sir.

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:06.700
You know perfectly well I can wear nothing with comfort until Sir Percy says so.

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:08.520
Where is that jack-and-apes?

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:09.300
Who, sir?

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:10.580
You, sir.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:11.770
Yes, sir.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:16.450
And now tell Tweedle what's wrong with this coat.

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:24.460
The back is admirable.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:26.320
Front's fair.

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:29.530
The collar is, er, passable.

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:35.630
But the sleeve, Treadle, the cuff, my poor benighted friend.

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:37.970
That, Sir Percy, is the last word in cuffs.

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:40.590
Oh, gad, I should hope so, for there should never be another lady.

00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.950
Oh, come now, it's not too bad.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.090
You see, Sir Percy, His Royal Highness approves.

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:48.490
My poor Treadle, His Royal Highness does nothing of the kind.

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:50.090
He says it's not so bad.

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:55.120
And nothing in the world is so bad as something which is not so bad.

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:55.820
But Sir Percy...

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:57.940
Oh, it's a crime, Treadle. Worse, a blunderer.

00:37:58.270 --> 00:37:59.970
And quite, quite fatal to my reputation.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:02.020
Why to your reputation?

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:06.040
Because all the world knows that His Royal Highness is guided by my taste.

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:08.500
Percy is an expert on coal.

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:12.400
And riches, sir. I'm a very wonder with the inexpressibles. Isn't that so, Treadle?

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:14.280
Well, yes. Up to a point.

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:17.020
Zounds, Treadle, why must you be so cursed jealous?

00:38:17.440 --> 00:38:21.840
Look at that puny sleeve, that miserable dishrag of lace. Odds fish.

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:23.440
Looks like the lining hanging down.

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:25.100
It was only intended for a plain cut.

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.120
Plain? It's as ugly as a parson's widow.

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:29.950
Open up your sleeve, man. Let your ruffles take...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.240
Let them flow, let them ripple, so that when his royal highness takes snuff,

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:36.500
it will be a swallow's flight.

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:44.450
That's it? Why, Demi Percy, you brainless, spineless, useless, but you do no clothe.

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:47.040
Odd's fish, that's something, isn't it, sir?

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:48.640
Tweedle, Tweedle.

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:49.340
Yes, your knight.

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:53.500
Don't forget what Sir Percy says about the sleeve, a swallow's flight.

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:54.020
Yes, your knight.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:55.420
Flight of a swallow.

00:38:56.530 --> 00:38:57.710
Come along, come along, gentlemen.

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:06.270
Oh, by the way, wouldn't I?

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:14.100
The show glance arrived in London.

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:17.950
I've been watched. I'm just off to the fight with the Prince.

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:19.640
Fought to me there.

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:20.180
Very good.

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:52.440
I wager 100 guineas, he won't last two more rounds.

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:13.260
Heads be damned, I'm double.

00:40:13.480 --> 00:40:13.740
Done!

00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:16.160
Zoran's pussy must be mad, Mendoza's finished!

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:23.350
Get up, Mendoza!

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:24.060
Where is he?

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:27.380
He's at your house.

00:40:30.380 --> 00:40:34.670
No, I will not trick me once into giving you information about the Marquis Sincere.

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:36.400
I shall never forgive myself.

00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:40.480
I'm sick of this sentimental twaddle about the poor, persecuted aristocrats.

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:44.140
Have you forgotten what they did to the defenseless peasantry for centuries?

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:47.840
What this very Sincere did to you?

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:53.340
No, I have not. But I'm no spy.

00:40:55.810 --> 00:40:56.790
That is your last word?

00:40:56.950 --> 00:40:57.390
Absolutely.

00:40:59.290 --> 00:40:59.750
I wonder.

00:41:01.180 --> 00:41:03.120
Oh, by the way, how is your brother, Armand?

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:06.460
Very well, I hope.

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:08.170
I hope so, too.

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:10.600
But yesterday, he was arrested.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:11.600
Arrested?

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:12.890
At Boulogne.

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:14.720
What for?

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:17.180
As a traitor to his country.

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:18.170
Traitor?

00:41:18.370 --> 00:41:24.700
You're lying, Chauveler.

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:27.230
Trying to trick me again.

00:41:27.810 --> 00:41:29.590
I have the proof of his guilt in my pocket.

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:34.940
This scrap of paper will certainly send your brother to the guillotine.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:37.690
Unless, of course, you care to redeem it.

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:41.990
Bring me the name of the Pimpernel and this is yours.

00:41:45.540 --> 00:41:48.550
If I wanted to, I don't know it.

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:49.110
Find it!

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:52.030
Can I if you can't with an army of spies?

00:41:52.270 --> 00:41:54.390
Because you go everywhere and know everyone in his circle.

00:41:55.020 --> 00:41:55.980
That much I do know.

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:59.980
And this also, that he will be among the guests tomorrow night.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.000
at the Grenville Ball.

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:02.320
So will half the world.

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:03.580
Everything is impossible.

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:08.800
To a clever woman whose brother's life is at stake,

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:09.920
nothing is impossible.

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:11.630
Well?

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:16.800
No, Chauvelin. I won't.

00:42:18.440 --> 00:42:19.080
Very well, madame.

00:42:35.370 --> 00:42:43.600
I swear to give me that paper.

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:48.530
The moment I catch the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:42:49.770 --> 00:42:53.630
Sounds, that name again? I've heard nothing else all day.

00:42:54.230 --> 00:42:55.910
At the club, the fight, and now here.

00:42:56.620 --> 00:42:58.380
I protest the fellas are public nuisance.

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:04.230
I beg your pardon, my dear. Do I intrude?

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.990
No, no. This is an old acquaintance,

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:10.820
Monsieur Chauvelin, the French ambassador, my husband.

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:13.360
Charmed, delighted, enchanted.

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:16.720
Devilish clever race, the French.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:20.350
How they speak that unspeakable language of theirs defeats me.

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:21.610
You flatter us, Sir Percy.

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:23.710
No, no, you've got the cleverest heads in the world.

00:43:24.100 --> 00:43:26.550
The only trouble is, you all go to pieces round the neck.

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:27.410
Round the neck?

00:43:28.120 --> 00:43:29.440
Yes, now look at that thing.

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:31.140
Think me, what a mess.

00:43:31.910 --> 00:43:34.810
Now, if you'd really like to know how to tie a cravat, I'll tell you.

00:43:35.050 --> 00:43:37.070
But it isn't easy, mind you. It takes all my brains.

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:37.990
I'm sure it would.

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:39.390
Yes. Well, now, look here.

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:42.670
Well, you see, first of all, the thing goes twice round the neck.

00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:45.920
And then the front folds back to allow the back to come to the front.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:49.340
Otherwise, the front would be all behind, as it was before.

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:51.500
Percy, what are you talking about?

00:43:51.820 --> 00:43:52.860
You don't follow me, my dear?

00:43:53.390 --> 00:43:56.130
That's exactly what I say. It takes brains, doesn't it?

00:43:56.470 --> 00:43:57.250
One can see that.

00:43:57.450 --> 00:43:58.490
Yes, of course.

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:02.900
Good day, Lady Blakeney.

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:03.780
Oh, no, no, don't go.

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:06.460
You and my wife must have so much to say to each other.

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:07.230
We have.

00:44:08.110 --> 00:44:10.990
But then I promise myself a little tete-a-tete with Lady Blakeney

00:44:10.990 --> 00:44:12.750
at Lord Grenfell's Ball tomorrow night.

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:15.600
Good. Tomorrow night, then.

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:19.920
But before you go, you must hear my verse about that cursed Pimpernel fellow.

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:20.560
You'll love it.

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:24.300
Listen, they seek him here. They seek him there.

00:44:24.840 --> 00:44:27.060
Those Frenchies seek him everywhere.

00:44:27.840 --> 00:44:29.980
Is he in heaven? Is he in heaven?

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.320
Hell, that dimmed, elusive, pimpernel.

00:44:33.790 --> 00:44:34.230
Delightful.

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:34.470
What?

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:38.470
Especially that line, those Frenchies seek him everywhere.

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:41.960
Yes, I like that too, because you see, I hear that they do,

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:45.010
and that gives the line a sort of something,

00:44:45.490 --> 00:44:47.670
that sort of gives it a something,

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:50.240
if I make myself clear.

00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:51.460
Clear as crystal.

00:44:52.730 --> 00:44:54.470
Good day, my dear Sir Pess.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:57.170
Bonjour, monsieur, as the French say.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:00.460
Bonjour.

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:09.500
Magnificent fight this afternoon, my dear.

00:45:11.210 --> 00:45:13.230
Glad that fellow Mendoza's got quick ears.

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:16.280
In the tenth round, when Jackson had him down, I shouted,

00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:20.270
Get up, Mendoza! And dammy, he did. And sink me, he won.

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:25.370
I think Andrew Fuchs might be the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:29.140
Andrew? Never.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.350
He couldn't hit a ball at Eton. Why?

00:45:33.320 --> 00:45:35.180
What's your interest in the Scarlet Pimpernel?

00:45:35.770 --> 00:45:38.070
No more than any other woman's. We'd all like to know who he is.

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:40.380
So would your friend Chauvelin, our wager.

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:41.340
What makes you say that?

00:45:41.820 --> 00:45:44.420
Isn't that what he's here for? Did he tell you?

00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:46.370
Why should he?

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:48.440
Why not? Mind you, the man's clever.

00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:51.000
But a fellow who can't even tie his own cravat

00:45:51.020 --> 00:45:54.040
isn't likely to put a noose round the Pimpernel's neck, is he?

00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:59.230
Really, Percy? Can you never rise above trivialities?

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:02.900
I can't rise above anything, more than three syllables, my dear, never could.

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:08.410
Nonsense. You were a man once, a man a woman could look up to,

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:15.750
turned to in trouble, and now I wouldn't know where to begin.

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:29.120
Don't you begin by telling me what trouble is?

00:46:30.670 --> 00:46:33.750
What is the use? We don't even speak the same language.

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:12.510
Well, here's our friend's friend. Bonsoir, monsieur, as you say.

00:47:12.970 --> 00:47:16.470
Good evening, sir. Lady?

00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:20.530
Dear, look at that crevette. You're a monstrous quick learner, sir.

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:26.210
Permit me. There. Perfect.

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.390
Another week and we won't know you from an Englishman.

00:47:29.530 --> 00:47:29.970
From an Englishman?

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:31.340
That is praise indeed.

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:36.320
What is this poem everybody is talking about?

00:47:36.560 --> 00:47:36.880
Poem?

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:38.840
Yes, all about the Scarlet Pimpernel.

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:42.000
Oh, Gad, there's no escape.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:45.740
Forgive me, my dear, take our friend round and tell him who everybody is.

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:47.620
If anybody is anybody.

00:47:53.080 --> 00:47:56.340
I only want to know who one person is, and you know the alternative.

00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:59.780
The Pimpernel is under this roof at this moment, among your friends.

00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:03.940
Your move, m'lady. Good luck.

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:27.240
The thing rhymes, you see, in four places.

00:48:27.770 --> 00:48:29.970
And if you can get a rhyme to rhyme...

00:48:30.220 --> 00:48:32.760
That makes it a poem, if you follow me.

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:34.580
Yes, of course, but what is it?

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:38.740
The Scarlet Pimpernel by Sir Percy Blakeney Baroness.

00:48:40.120 --> 00:48:41.540
That's only the name.

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:45.980
They seek him here, they seek him there.

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:48.320
Those Frenchies seek him everywhere.

00:48:49.130 --> 00:48:49.970
Is he in heaven?

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:51.840
Is he in...

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:56.770
That dimmed, illusive Pimpernel?

00:48:57.510 --> 00:48:59.980
Oh, yes, it's...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:00.480
I read a poem!

00:49:34.220 --> 00:49:35.620
Who made that?

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:37.000
Beagle and Beagle.

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:39.960
Oh, it's blood. Sink me if they're not better than old master Tweedles.

00:49:40.160 --> 00:49:41.260
The folks in Dewhurst are free.

00:49:41.740 --> 00:49:42.100
Escaped?

00:49:42.200 --> 00:49:43.960
No, released. They're coming on here immediately.

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:45.880
Of course. That's why they were released.

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:49.860
Need a trail to the rest of us. I shall be in the library at midnight.

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:55.720
His Royal Highness, the Prince of Wales!

00:50:06.010 --> 00:50:26.640
Ladies, please, gentlemen, please, ah, good evening, ladies, gentlemen, gentlemen, please, please, any news from France, Grenoble?

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:28.530
The worst, sir. The king doomed.

00:50:31.150 --> 00:50:31.730
Can't we do anything?

00:50:32.340 --> 00:50:33.980
We recalled our ambassador months ago.

00:50:34.580 --> 00:50:36.740
Chauvelin had the impudence to accept my invitation.

00:50:38.810 --> 00:50:39.710
Will Your Highness permit?

00:50:40.390 --> 00:50:43.490
Monsieur Chauvelin, the unofficial ambassador of the French government.

00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:49.010
Monsieur, we will try to forget the government who sent you

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:51.440
and look upon you merely as our guest,

00:50:52.310 --> 00:50:53.650
a private gentleman from France.

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:55.870
As such, are you welcome, Monsieur.

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:58.980
Well, ladies, if you back my horse, you might.

00:51:11.900 --> 00:51:17.140
I didn't want to say, Blakely with a sad face.

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:20.240
Oh, but sadness is for mere mortals, milady.

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:21.930
Never, never for goddess.

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:25.940
Alas, even a goddess must have moments when she's just a plain woman.

00:51:26.370 --> 00:51:27.210
Sounds, madam.

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:29.970
Lady Blakely could never become a plain woman at any...

00:51:30.410 --> 00:51:34.890
That's better. Now come and help me through my princely paces.

00:51:35.790 --> 00:51:38.230
I crave your permission to present to your royal highness...

00:51:38.230 --> 00:51:40.320
...the Quantes de Torrenay, her daughter.

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:41.460
This is a pleasure, madam.

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:44.140
Ah, charming. Charming.

00:51:44.740 --> 00:51:47.320
You and Lady Blakely must become great friends, madam.

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:49.680
Her friends are my friends.

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:53.300
And her enemies, the enemies of England.

00:51:54.200 --> 00:51:57.020
We pour yourselves, madam, to show our gratitude to England...

00:51:57.080 --> 00:51:59.260
...by our devotion to the wishes of Monsignor.

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:10.810
They were schoolfellows in the convent in Paris.

00:52:10.990 --> 00:52:12.550
Ah, charming, madame.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:16.780
Your old highness, I accepted Lord Grenville's gracious invitation...

00:52:17.720 --> 00:52:21.160
solely to implore your highness to do something to save my husband.

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:26.330
Madame, the government does everything in its power...

00:52:26.740 --> 00:52:29.560
save those who are threatened by death in the prisons of the French Republic.

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:36.430
But if a country goes mad, it has the right to commit every horror within its own walls.

00:52:36.630 --> 00:52:43.940
Let's lift a note into Sir Andrew Pooce's cover.

00:52:45.410 --> 00:52:46.190
Thank you, sir.

00:52:55.540 --> 00:52:59.550
Ah, Lady Pooce, won't you take pity on a lonely compatriot?

00:53:03.420 --> 00:53:04.720
Are you asking me to dance with you?

00:53:06.260 --> 00:53:09.370
Not with me. Mr. Andrew Fruits.

00:53:10.150 --> 00:53:10.550
Why?

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:12.660
He has a note in his cup. Get it.

00:53:15.340 --> 00:53:16.140
Listen, Chauvelin.

00:53:16.280 --> 00:53:16.760
Get it.

00:53:44.110 --> 00:53:48.490
Gentlemen, this is for the minuet.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:30.480
Can you see me upstairs?

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:31.320
Of course.

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:46.160
Where, Lady Blakeney?

00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:46.960
You'll be all right in a moment.

00:54:58.700 --> 00:54:59.360
Lady Blakeney!

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:00.820
Shall I send for a physician?

00:55:01.700 --> 00:55:06.200
No, no. I'm so sorry.

00:55:07.930 --> 00:55:08.430
I'll be all right.

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:14.790
Would you rather be left alone?

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:16.820
Please don't leave me.

00:55:16.960 --> 00:55:20.720
Just for a second.

00:55:21.020 --> 00:55:27.260
I'll be all right.

00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:27.770
Oh, how clever of you. Almost as good as a burnt feather for faintness.

00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:29.540
There.

00:56:30.700 --> 00:56:35.010
I feel better already. Would you be kind enough to bring me my fan?

00:56:35.190 --> 00:56:59.450
It was a love letter, and you sacrificed it for me.

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:03.990
What a shame. But I'll ask her to write you another. Shall I?

00:57:05.710 --> 00:57:07.670
Suzanne, are you feeling better, darling?

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:13.720
I spoiled Sir Andrew's dance. Would you make it up to him by finishing it for me?

00:57:33.130 --> 00:57:33.370
You ready?

00:57:34.660 --> 00:57:35.240
He burnt it.

00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:37.980
I got a glimpse of what was left.

00:57:39.220 --> 00:57:39.640
It began.

00:57:40.660 --> 00:57:41.500
Well, go on.

00:57:43.390 --> 00:57:49.140
Start tomorrow myself and end it will be in the library at midnight.

01:00:32.680 --> 01:00:33.080
Thank you.

01:01:33.710 --> 01:01:34.890
Thank you, man. Go to bed.

